Lecture 5 — Building a Vector Storage
Goal of this lecture: understand how text becomes vectors, how to measure the similarity between two vectors with cosine similarity, why we store those vectors in a specialised index (pgvector), and how to chunk documents and attach metadata so they can be retrieved.
By the end you should be able to embed and index a set of documents and run a nearest-neighbor semantic search — the retrieval foundation that Lecture 6 (Retrieval-Augmented Generation) is built on.

1. Why vectors? Meaning as geometry
A language model only knows what it was trained on. Your company handbook, your course PDFs, last week's meeting notes — none of it is in the model's weights. To let an LLM answer questions about your data, you first need a way to find the relevant pieces of that data for a given question. That is the job of a vector store.

Plain keyword search is not enough. If a user asks "How do I reset my password?" but the document says "changing your login credentials", a keyword match finds nothing — there are no shared words. We need search that understands meaning, not just spelling.

The trick is to turn each piece of text into a vector: a fixed-length list of numbers. A good embedding places texts that mean similar things close together in this space, and unrelated texts far apart.

"a cat sat on the mat"  →  [0.12, -0.04, 0.88, ... ]   # e.g. 1024 numbers (bge-m3)
The direction a vector points encodes its meaning. The classic illustration is that vector arithmetic captures relationships: 
