king−man+woman≈queen, because the difference "man → woman" points in a similar direction to "king → queen". You will not do that arithmetic in practice, but it captures the core idea: meaning has become geometry, and geometry is something a computer can measure.

2. Embedding models
An embedding model is what produces these vectors. You give it a string, it returns a vector (its embedding). Unlike the chat models from Lecture 1, an embedding model does not generate text — it only maps text to numbers.

Three ways to get embeddings:

Hosted APIs. OpenAI (text-embedding-3-small, 1536 dimensions), Cohere, and Voyage all expose an embeddings endpoint. One HTTP call returns a vector.
Local models. The sentence-transformers library runs models like all-MiniLM-L6-v2 (384 dimensions) on your own machine — no API cost, your data never leaves your server.
Our class model. The class LLM proxy serves bge-m3 (1024 dimensions), a strong multilingual model (good on English and Thai). It speaks the same OpenAI API as the chat models, so the same client.embeddings.create(...) call works — and unlike the others below, this one runs in the class sandbox and from your own machine with your personal key.
The call is identical regardless of provider — only the model name changes. This one is runnable right here:

from openai import OpenAI

client = OpenAI()   # in the sandbox the proxy URL + key are already set
vec = client.embeddings.create(
    model="bge-m3",
    input="a cat sat on the mat",
).data[0].embedding
print("dimensions:", len(vec))   # -> 1024
print("first 5:", [round(x, 3) for x in vec[:5]])
The hosted/local variants look the same — swap the model and (for local) the library:

# Hosted OpenAI:  model="text-embedding-3-small"  -> 1536 floats
# Local:
from sentence_transformers import SentenceTransformer
model = SentenceTransformer("all-MiniLM-L6-v2")
vec = model.encode("a cat sat on the mat")   # -> numpy array of 384 floats
Batch your calls. Embedding one string at a time is slow and wasteful. Every real API takes a list and returns one vector per item — pass input=[chunk1, chunk2, …] and read resp.data[i].embedding for the i-th chunk. A 300-page PDF might be thousands of chunks; batch them (a few hundred per call) instead of firing thousands of requests.

Two rules that trip people up:

Dimensionality is fixed per model. Every vector from bge-m3 has exactly 1024 numbers (and every text-embedding-3-small vector exactly 1536). You cannot mix vectors of different lengths.
You can only compare vectors from the same model. A vector from bge-m3 and a vector from MiniLM live in different spaces and are not comparable. Pick one embedding model for a project and use it for both your documents and your queries.
Meaning crosses languages
bge-m3 is multilingual — that's why we picked it. It places a sentence and its translation near each other even though they share not one character. For a Thai-and-English cohort this is the feature: retrieval over Thai course notes with an English question (or the reverse) just works. Run it in the sandbox:

import math
from openai import OpenAI

client = OpenAI()

texts = [
    "how do I reset my password",          # EN query
    "ฉันจะรีเซ็ตรหัสผ่านได้อย่างไร",          # same meaning, Thai
    "the cat sat on the mat",              # unrelated EN
    "ตลาดหุ้นร่วงลงอย่างหนักวันนี้",          # unrelated Thai (stock market fell)
]

def cosine(a, b):
    dot = sum(x * y for x, y in zip(a, b))
    na = math.sqrt(sum(x * x for x in a))
    nb = math.sqrt(sum(y * y for y in b))
    return dot / (na * nb) if na and nb else 0.0

resp = client.embeddings.create(model="bge-m3", input=texts)
vecs = [d.embedding for d in resp.data]
q = vecs[0]   # the English password question
for text, v in sorted(zip(texts[1:], vecs[1:]), key=lambda p: -cosine(q, p[1])):
    print(f"{cosine(q, v):.3f}  {text}")
The Thai "reset my password" line scores ~0.90 — far above the unrelated Thai (~0.49) and English (~0.34) sentences. The model matched meaning across a language barrier, with zero shared characters. Same model="bge-m3", same cosine; only the text changed. (Note the unrelated lines still sit around 0.3–0.5, not 0: real embedding spaces are crowded, so you rank — take the highest — rather than trust an absolute threshold.)

