Answers
With the query "who barked at the mailman", the dog sentence shares the content words barked, at, the, mailman, so it ranks first for the right reason. (Contrast the old query "a puppy was loud near the house": it would still beat the others, but only on the shared stopword a — another bag-of-words weakness, since stopwords carry no meaning yet sway the score.) The cats sentence still scores 0 because bag-of-words only matches identical tokens, and it shares none with either query — the exact gap a trained embedding model closes.
Replace ranked[0] with a slice: sort as before, then iterate ranked[:2] (the VectorStore.search(query_vec, k=2) in §4 does exactly this).
With overlap=0 the windows are disjoint, so any sentence spanning a boundary is split in two; raising overlap to a few words makes consecutive chunks share their edges, and the split sentence reappears whole in one of them. (The final chunk is always complete — it's never truncated, just possibly short.)
A chunks table with embedding vector(1024) plus content, source, page; the query adds WHERE source = $1 before ORDER BY embedding <=> $2 LIMIT k (the metadata filter narrows the scan, then cosine distance ranks).
delete(id) rebuilds self.items without that id; the filter passes a source into search() and skips items whose meta["source"] does not match before scoring.
The two orders are always identical because distance = 1 − similarity is monotonically decreasing in similarity — the highest similarity is the lowest distance, for the same pair. pgvector's <=> returns distance, so you ORDER BY embedding <=> $1 ascending (LIMIT k) to get the nearest first.
With overlap=0 the splits are [w1..w6] [w7..w12] [w13..w14], stranding "...to you" (the emailed link) away from "reset your password." overlap=2 is enough: chunk 3 becomes [w9..w14] ("...follow the link emailed to you") while chunk 1 still holds the reset action — but if your query needs both in one chunk you must overlap further or use a larger size. The overlap turned 3 chunks into 3 here (short text); on longer text expect ~10–30% more chunks.
